{"@context":["https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/ns/content/v1.jsonld",{"@base":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio/","folio-assistant-sci":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant-sci/v0/ns/"}],"@id":"papers/fred2005-formal-groups/blocks/thm-skew-poisson-integrates","@type":["folio-assistant-sci:Theorem","doco:Section"],"label":"thm:skew-poisson-integrates","kind":"theorem","title":"Integration theorem","uses":["papers/fred2005-formal-groups/blocks/def-commutative-formal-group","papers/fred2005-formal-groups/blocks/def-skewed-poisson-structure","papers/fred2005-formal-groups/blocks/def-strong-multiplicativity","papers/fred2005-formal-groups/blocks/def-non-commutative-formal-group"],"cites":["references/leitner2006","references/fred2005"],"tags":["integration","formal-group","main-theorem"],"leanRef":"fred2005:Fred2005.integration_commutator","text":"skew-poisson-integrates.md","leanSource":"skew-poisson-integrates.lean","provenance":"authored","html":"<a id=\"thm:skew-poisson-integrates\"></a><p>Let <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">k</code> be a field of characteristic <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">p > 0</code>. A formal group <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">G</code> over <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">k</code>\nis <strong>geometric</strong> if there are commutative formal subgroups <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">H_i \\subseteq G</code>,\n<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">i \\in I</code>, with <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">G \\cong \\prod_i H_i</code> as formal varieties; its underlying\ncommutative group is <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">H = \\prod_i H_i</code> (<span class=\"cite\" data-keys=\"leitner2006\">[leitner2006]</span>, Definition 3.1).</p>\n<p><strong>Theorem</strong> (<span class=\"cite\" data-keys=\"leitner2006\">[leitner2006]</span>, Theorem 3.7). Let <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\{H_i, H\\}</code> be a\ncommutative geometric formal group over <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">k</code>, and let <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\pi</code> be a strongly\nfiltered, <a href=\"#def:strong-multiplicativity\" data-at=\"formal-groups-chapter/skewed-poisson\">strongly multiplicative</a>\n<a href=\"#def:skewed-poisson-structure\" data-at=\"formal-groups-chapter/skewed-poisson\">Poisson structure</a> on the splay <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">T</code> of the\n<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathrm{Dist}(H_i)</code>. Let <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">J \\subseteq T</code> be the ideal</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-math math-display\">  J = \\bigl(fg - gf - \\pi(f, g) \\;\\big|\\; f, g \\in T\\bigr).</code></pre>\n<p>Then <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">U = T / J</code> is the distribution algebra of a\n<a href=\"#def:non-commutative-formal-group\" data-at=\"formal-groups-chapter/commutative-formal-group\">formal group</a> <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">G</code>, and <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\{H_i, G\\}</code> is a\ngeometric formal group. A Poisson morphism of such splays induces a\nmorphism of the resulting geometric formal groups.</p>\n<p>Conversely, a geometric formal group <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\{H_i, G\\}</code> gives a strongly\nfiltered, strongly multiplicative Poisson structure on the splay of the\n<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathrm{Dist}(H_i)</code> (<span class=\"cite\" data-keys=\"leitner2006\">[leitner2006]</span>, Lemma 3.5), independent of choices\nup to equivalence (Proposition 3.6). The commutator bracket <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\pi_c</code> returns\n<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">U \\cong T</code> unchanged.</p>\n<p>What is formalised: the construction <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">U = T / J</code> and the identity\n<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">[f, g]_U = \\pi(f, g)</code> in <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">U</code>. What is cited: that <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">U</code> is\n<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathrm{Dist}(G)</code> of a formal group.</p>\n<p>Reference: <span class=\"cite\" data-keys=\"leitner2006\">[leitner2006]</span>, §3.</p>\n","source":"folio/fred2005-formal-groups/formal-groups-chapter/skew-poisson-integrates.md","heading":"Theorem","number":"1.4.1","page":"b/thm-skew-poisson-integrates","qa":{"counts":{"pass":72,"fail":0,"warn":3,"na":21,"unknown":0,"stale":52}},"lean":{"expected":true,"ref":"fred2005:Fred2005.integration_commutator","via":"sibling","path":"folio/fred2005-formal-groups/formal-groups-chapter/skew-poisson-integrates.lean","compiles":"unchecked","sorry":false,"sorryBasis":"text"}}
